N2  - Summary Over the past six decades, hundreds of microsatellites, nanosatellites and even picosatellites have been launched for educational, experimental and operational purposes. CubeSat projects are small-scale, low-cost, multidisciplinary projects where student teams can learn and apply systems engineering. Systems engineering is the primary means of determining whether and how the challenge posed by a program's requirements can be met with the resources available. This chapter explains the Key satellite design parameters, and CubeSat design requirements and constraints. A CubeSat must meet certain criteria in terms of shape, size and weight. The general architecture of a CubeSat or satellite is divided into two parts: the platform and the payload. Satellite architecture must be rigorously analyzed and designed to withstand the mechanical launch environment. The chapter also summarizes the various methods of solving partial differential equations for analyzing mechanical systems under stress.
